"""The German court table — every court the corpus can hold a decision from.

German judgments carry no neutral citation. A decision is identified by its **ECLI**
(``ECLI:DE:OVGNRW:2024:0115.13A1234.20.00``) or, where no ECLI was minted, by *court +
Aktenzeichen* ("OVG Münster, Beschl. v. 15.1.2024 – 13 A 1234/20"). Both routes need the
same thing: one canonical identity per court, reachable from every spelling a citing
judgment might use. That is what this table is.

Three jobs, and the table is shaped by all three:

**1. A name for every court.** ``documents.court`` holds the full German name, and the UI
renders it verbatim (see ``Facade.court_label``). 918 distinct courts appear in the
openlegaldata bulk alone — from the Bundesgerichtshof down to the Amtsgericht Hünfeld —
and an unregistered one falls through to a prettifier that splits on hyphens, turning
"Oberverwaltungsgericht Nordrhein-Westfalen" into "Oberverwaltungsgericht Nordrhein
Westfalen". Every court therefore has a row.

**2. An ECLI court token → the court.** The token is the third ECLI field, and it is what
an ``ECLI:DE:`` citation resolves through. It is NOT unique: the specialist tribunals sit
at, and mint ECLIs under, their host court — the Anwaltsgerichtshof Nordrhein-Westfalen
issues ``ECLI:DE:OLGHAM:…``, the Berufsgericht für Heilberufe Berlin ``ECLI:DE:VGBE:…``,
the Schifffahrtsobergericht Berlin ``ECLI:DE:KG:…``. So a token maps to the PRIMARY court
(the ordinary court of that seat), and the tribunals keep their own rows and their own
names, reachable by name rather than by token.

**3. One key per court, from any spelling.** ``court_key`` folds "OVG Münster", "OVG
Nordrhein-Westfalen", "Oberverwaltungsgericht NRW" and "ECLI:DE:OVGNRW" onto ``OVGNRW``,
so the ``de:case:OVGNRW:13A1234/20`` alias a citation mints and the one the harvested
decision registers are the same string.

## The ambiguity gate on seat/state spellings

The upstream alias lists are machine-generated and pair each court's type with both its
SEAT and its Bundesland. The seat forms are what German lawyers actually write and are
almost always unique ("OVG Münster", "VG Köln"). The state forms are one-to-many and
would mislink wholesale: ten NRW Verwaltungsgerichte each claim "VG Nordrhein-Westfalen",
three NRW Oberlandesgerichte each claim "OLG Nordrhein-Westfalen". So generated spellings
are admitted **only where they name exactly one court in the whole table** — the same
distinctiveness rule the shorthand store applies to names (see
``citations.extractor.valid_shorthand``). "OVG Nordrhein-Westfalen" survives (NRW has one
OVG); "VG Nordrhein-Westfalen" does not.

Regenerated from the openlegaldata court register (which carries the ECLI codes) unioned
with the courts the bulk dump itself carries; the two disagree on ~50 courts, mostly
because the register's names were OCR'd from a directory ("Amtsgericht Biidingen" for
Büdingen), and the better-spelled name wins.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import re
import unicodedata
from dataclasses import dataclass
from functools import lru_cache


@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class GermanCourt:
    #: the ECLI court token — third field of ``ECLI:DE:<code>:…``. Not unique: a
    #: specialist tribunal mints ECLIs under the court it sits at.
    code: str
    #: the name the UI shows; never an abbreviation
    name: str
    #: AG | LG | OLG | VG | OVG | VGH | SG | LSG | ARBG | LAG | FG | … ("" if untyped)
    court_type: str
    #: Gerichtsbarkeit — the branch (ordentliche, Verwaltungs-, Sozial-, Arbeits-, Finanz-)
    jurisdiction: str
    #: Sachliche Zuständigkeit (Amtsgericht, Landgericht, Oberlandesgericht, Bundesgericht)
    level: str
    #: Bundesland, or "Bundesrepublik Deutschland" for a federal court
    state: str
    #: the openlegaldata slug — the API's own key, and how the adapter looks a court up
    slug: str
    #: seat/state spellings this court is cited by, before the uniqueness gate
    places: tuple[str, ...] = ()


# Court-type abbreviation → the word it stands for. Drives both the spellings
# ``court_key`` accepts and the citation regex ``COURT_RE``, so it must cover every
# ``court_type`` used below.
TYPE_WORDS: dict[str, str] = {
    "AG": "Amtsgericht", "LG": "Landgericht", "OLG": "Oberlandesgericht",
    "KG": "Kammergericht", "OBLG": "Bayerisches Oberstes Landesgericht",
    "VG": "Verwaltungsgericht", "OVG": "Oberverwaltungsgericht",
    "VGH": "Verwaltungsgerichtshof", "LVG": "Landesverwaltungsgericht",
    "SG": "Sozialgericht", "LSG": "Landessozialgericht",
    "ARBG": "Arbeitsgericht", "LAG": "Landesarbeitsgericht",
    "FG": "Finanzgericht", "VERFG": "Verfassungsgericht",
    "STGH": "Staatsgerichtshof", "AWG": "Anwaltsgericht", "AWGH": "Anwaltsgerichtshof",
    "LBGH": "Landesberufsgericht für Heilberufe", "SCHG": "Schiedsgericht",
    "SCHOG": "Schifffahrtsobergericht", "MSCHOG": "Moselschifffahrtsobergericht",
    "RSCHOG": "Rheinschifffahrtsobergericht", "RSCHGD": "Rheinschifffahrtsgericht",
    "BGH": "Bundesgerichtshof", "BAG": "Bundesarbeitsgericht", "BFH": "Bundesfinanzhof",
    "BSG": "Bundessozialgericht", "BVerwG": "Bundesverwaltungsgericht",
    "BVerfG": "Bundesverfassungsgericht", "BPatG": "Bundespatentgericht",
}

#: Courts with no row of their own below, because they are not German courts — but a
#: German judgment cites them constantly and a reader needs the name. The CJEU/General
#: Court/ECtHR decisions themselves are held under their own EU/CoE sources.
FOREIGN_COURT_NAMES: dict[str, str] = {
    "EUGH": "Gerichtshof der Europäischen Union",
    "EUG": "Gericht der Europäischen Union",
    "EGMR": "Europäischer Gerichtshof für Menschenrechte",
    "EPA": "Europäisches Patentamt",
}

